{{smaller|where <math>\text{U}</math> is the velocity in free air, <math>\text{R}</math> is the radius of the pipe, <math>\text{N}</math> the frequency, and <math>\rho</math> the air density. <math>\text{C}</math> is a constant, equal to the coefficient of viscosity in Helmholtz's theory, but less simple in Kirchhoff's theory. Experiments on the velocity in pipes were carried out by H. Schneebeli (''Pogg. Ann.'', 1869, 136, p. 296) and by T. J. Seebeck (''Pogg. Ann.'', 1870, 139, p. 104) which accorded with this result as far as <math>\text{R}</math> is concerned, but the diminution of velocity was found to be more nearly proportional to <math>\text{N}^{-\frac{3}{4}}</math>. Kundt also obtained results in general agreement with the formula (Rayleigh, ''Sound'', ii. § 260). He used his dust-tube method.}}

{{c|''Elementary Theory of the Transverse Vibration of Musical Strings.''}}

We shall first investigate the velocity with which a disturbance travels along a string of mass <math>m</math> per unit length when it is stretched with a constant tension <math>\text{T}</math>, the same at all points. We shall then show that on certain limitations two trains of disturbance may be superposed so that stationary waves may be formed, and thence we shall deduce the modes of vibration as with pipes.
